1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,560
This is MATH 239, video 10a. The topic is compositions. This video uses the Sum, Product and String Lemmas from video 9.

2
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:16,791
After this video, you can define a composition, its parts, its length and its size.

3
00:00:17,241 --> 00:00:24,132
You can write a set of compositions with the star operation, or as a union of Cartesian products.

4
00:00:24,582 --> 00:00:29,211
You can prove that there are 2 to the n minus 1 compositions of n.

5
00:00:29,661 --> 00:00:35,080
And you can find the generating series when the parts or the lengths have restrictions.

6
00:00:35,530 --> 00:00:41,951
Here is the problem. In how many ways can we write n as an ordered sum of positive integers?

7
00:00:42,401 --> 00:00:46,604
Take n equal to 6. Draw it as a row of six unit cells.

8
00:00:47,054 --> 00:00:51,193
Cut the row into consecutive pieces. Here we make two cuts.

9
00:00:51,643 --> 00:01:00,773
The pieces have 3, 1 and 2 cells. So the parts are c 1 equal to 3, c 2 equal to 1, and c 3 equal to 2.

10
00:01:01,223 --> 00:01:08,626
A composition is such a list, gamma, from c 1 to c k. Each part c i is a positive integer.

11
00:01:09,076 --> 00:01:15,348
The length of a composition is the number of parts. This one has 3 parts, so its length is 3.

12
00:01:15,798 --> 00:01:20,278
The size is the sum of the parts. 3 plus 1 plus 2 is 6.

13
00:01:20,728 --> 00:01:27,853
The order of the parts matters. 3, 1, 2 and 1, 2, 3 are different compositions of 6.

14
00:01:28,303 --> 00:01:37,605
Exactly one composition has no parts. It is the empty composition, epsilon. Its length is 0, and its size is 0.

15
00:01:38,055 --> 00:01:45,308
Now list all compositions of 5, sorted by length. The top row shows 5 as five unit cells.

16
00:01:45,758 --> 00:01:49,726
Length 1: one composition. Its only part is 5.

17
00:01:50,176 --> 00:01:55,339
Length 2: four compositions. 1 4, 2 3, 3 2 and 4 1.

18
00:01:55,789 --> 00:01:58,050
Length 3: six compositions.

19
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:05,284
Length 4: four compositions. Each has three parts equal to 1 and one part equal to 2.

20
00:02:05,734 --> 00:02:09,553
Length 5: one composition. All five parts are 1.

21
00:02:10,003 --> 00:02:16,851
Add the counts. 1 plus 4 plus 6 plus 4 plus 1 is 16. That is 2 to the 4.

22
00:02:17,301 --> 00:02:25,130
The counts are the binomial coefficients 4 choose 0 to 4 choose 4. The next slides prove both facts.

23
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:34,433
Let P be the set of positive integers: 1, 2, 3, and so on. One part of a composition is one element of P.

24
00:02:34,883 --> 00:02:39,534
P to the 0 has one element: the empty composition, epsilon.

25
00:02:39,984 --> 00:02:46,683
P to the 1 is the set of lists with one element of P. These are the compositions with one part.

26
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:53,597
P to the 2 is the set of pairs c 1, c 2. These are the compositions with two parts.

27
00:02:54,047 --> 00:03:03,796
In general, a composition of length k is a list of k elements of P. So it is an element of the Cartesian product P to the k.

28
00:03:04,246 --> 00:03:08,811
P to the 3 holds the compositions with three parts, and so on.

29
00:03:09,261 --> 00:03:18,840
The length can be any natural number. So the set C of all compositions is the union of all P to the k. This union is P star.

30
00:03:19,290 --> 00:03:24,303
The union is disjoint, because each composition has exactly one length.

31
00:03:24,753 --> 00:03:30,897
Now the generating series. The weight of a composition is its size, the sum of its parts.

32
00:03:31,347 --> 00:03:44,894
One part is an element of P. Its weight is its value. So phi of P is x plus x squared plus x cubed, and so on. The blue row shows a 1 at every power from x to the 1.

33
00:03:45,344 --> 00:03:52,171
Take out x. The rest is a geometric series. So phi of P is x over 1 minus x.

34
00:03:52,621 --> 00:04:05,655
The weight of a list is the sum of the weights of its parts. So the Product Lemma gives phi of P to the k: x over 1 minus x, to the power k. These are the compositions of length k.

35
00:04:06,105 --> 00:04:14,340
The x to the 0 cell of phi of P is 0. So P has no element of weight 0, and the String Lemma applies.

36
00:04:14,790 --> 00:04:21,979
C is P star. So by the String Lemma, phi of C is 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x.

37
00:04:22,429 --> 00:04:29,469
The orange row is 1 minus phi of P. Its first cell is 1. Every other cell is minus 1.

38
00:04:29,919 --> 00:04:39,135
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x. The bottom is 1 minus x, minus x, which is 1 minus 2 x.

39
00:04:39,585 --> 00:04:47,073
Write the top as 1 minus 2 x, plus x. So phi of C is 1 plus x over 1 minus 2 x.

40
00:04:47,523 --> 00:04:59,662
x over 1 minus 2 x is x plus 2 x squared plus 4 x cubed, and so on. So the green row of phi of C is 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16.

41
00:05:00,112 --> 00:05:16,901
Check that the green row is the inverse of the orange row. Take the coefficient of x cubed in the product: 1 times 4, minus 1 times 2, minus 1 times 1, minus 1 times 1. This is 0, as it must be.

42
00:05:17,351 --> 00:05:28,445
Here is the String Lemma as a table. Row k is phi of P, to the power k. Row 0 is the series 1. It counts only the empty composition.

43
00:05:28,895 --> 00:05:34,527
Row 1 is x over 1 minus x. It counts the compositions with one part.

44
00:05:34,977 --> 00:05:40,310
Row 2 is the square of row 1. It counts the compositions with two parts.

45
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:45,325
Row 3 is the cube. It counts the compositions with three parts.

46
00:05:45,775 --> 00:05:55,226
The purple cells are the first nonzero cells. Row k starts at x to the k, because each of the k parts has size at least 1.

47
00:05:55,676 --> 00:06:05,340
The cell in row k and column x to the n counts the compositions of n with length k. Row 3, column x to the 5 shows 6.

48
00:06:05,790 --> 00:06:08,307
Rows 4 and 5 continue the pattern.

49
00:06:08,757 --> 00:06:17,440
Each entry is n minus 1 choose k minus 1. For row 3 and column x to the 5, 4 choose 2 is 6.

50
00:06:17,890 --> 00:06:26,082
The String Lemma adds all the rows. The green row holds the column sums: 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16.

51
00:06:26,532 --> 00:06:33,465
Check column x to the 5. 0 plus 1 plus 4 plus 6 plus 4 plus 1 is 16.

52
00:06:33,915 --> 00:06:47,441
These sums are the coefficients of 1 plus x over 1 minus 2 x. So there are 2 to the n minus 1 compositions of n, for n at least 1. For n equal to 0, there is 1.

53
00:06:47,891 --> 00:06:54,525
Here is a second proof, with a bijection. Write n as n unit cells in a row. Here n is 5.

54
00:06:54,975 --> 00:07:00,138
Between the cells there are n minus 1 gaps. 5 cells have 4 gaps.

55
00:07:00,588 --> 00:07:05,516
At each gap, we cut or we do not cut. So each gap has 2 choices.

56
00:07:05,966 --> 00:07:12,963
The 4 gaps give 2 times 2 times 2 times 2 choices. That is 2 to the 4, which is 16.

57
00:07:13,413 --> 00:07:19,920
Each set of cuts gives one composition. Cuts at gaps 2 and 3 give the parts 2, 1 and 2.

58
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:34,770
Each composition gives back its set of cuts. So the compositions of n match the subsets of the n minus 1 gaps, and there are 2 to the n minus 1. The notes prove this in Proposition 2.23.

59
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:49,791
A composition of length k has k minus 1 cuts. So n minus 1 choose k minus 1 compositions of n have length k. For n equal to 5 and k equal to 3, 4 choose 2 is 6.

60
00:07:50,241 --> 00:08:03,638
Most exam questions restrict the compositions. The notes use a method in three steps. Step 1. Find the allowed values for one part. This gives a set P and its series phi of P.

61
00:08:04,088 --> 00:08:09,443
Step 2. Find the allowed lengths. If any length is allowed, the set is P star.

62
00:08:09,893 --> 00:08:13,605
If the length must be even, the set is P squared, star.

63
00:08:14,055 --> 00:08:17,831
If the length is exactly k, the set is P to the k.

64
00:08:18,281 --> 00:08:29,289
Step 3. Apply the Sum, Product and String Lemmas, and name each lemma in your written solution. The next slides use this method on the examples of the notes.

65
00:08:29,739 --> 00:08:38,891
Example 2.18. F is the set of compositions with each part equal to 1 or 2. Find its generating series.

66
00:08:39,341 --> 00:08:47,362
Step 1. The allowed parts are 1 and 2. So P is the set 1, 2, and phi of P is x plus x squared.

67
00:08:47,812 --> 00:08:51,503
Step 2. Any length is allowed. So F is P star.

68
00:08:51,953 --> 00:09:01,702
Step 3. Phi of P has no constant term, so the String Lemma applies. Phi of F is 1 over 1 minus x minus x squared.

69
00:09:02,152 --> 00:09:12,157
Check the answer with n equal to 4. These are the 5 compositions of 4 with parts 1 or 2. The orange blocks are parts equal to 2.

70
00:09:12,607 --> 00:09:26,922
Now expand the series. Multiply both sides by 1 minus x minus x squared, and compare the coefficients. f 0 is 1, f 1 is 1, and each f n is the sum of the two before it.

71
00:09:27,372 --> 00:09:40,236
At x to the 4: f 2 plus f 3 is 2 plus 3, which is 5. This matches the 5 compositions in the list. Chapter 4 finds an exact formula for f n.

72
00:09:40,686 --> 00:09:46,830
Example 2.19. H is the set of compositions with each part at least 2.

73
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:54,533
The allowed parts are 2, 3, 4, and so on. In the blue row, every cell from x squared on is 1.

74
00:09:54,983 --> 00:10:05,927
So phi of P is x squared plus x cubed, and so on. Take out x squared. By the geometric series, this is x squared over 1 minus x.

75
00:10:06,377 --> 00:10:14,377
Any length is allowed. So by the String Lemma, phi of H is 1 over 1 minus x squared over 1 minus x.

76
00:10:14,827 --> 00:10:22,593
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x. The bottom becomes 1 minus x, minus x squared.

77
00:10:23,043 --> 00:10:34,093
Write the top as 1 minus x minus x squared, plus x squared. So phi of H is 1 plus x squared over 1 minus x minus x squared.

78
00:10:34,543 --> 00:10:40,303
Example 2.20. J is the set of compositions with each part odd.

79
00:10:40,753 --> 00:10:47,111
The allowed parts are 1, 3, 5, and so on. In the purple row, every odd cell is 1.

80
00:10:47,561 --> 00:10:56,905
So phi of P is x plus x cubed plus x to the 5, and so on. Take out x. This is x over 1 minus x squared.

81
00:10:57,355 --> 00:11:03,413
By the String Lemma, phi of J is 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x squared.

82
00:11:03,863 --> 00:11:12,375
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x squared. The bottom becomes 1 minus x squared, minus x.

83
00:11:12,825 --> 00:11:22,916
Write the top as 1 minus x minus x squared, plus x. So phi of J is 1 plus x over 1 minus x minus x squared.

84
00:11:23,366 --> 00:11:33,926
Phi of F from Example 2.18 has the same denominator: 1 minus x minus x squared. The next slide shows what this means.

85
00:11:34,376 --> 00:11:41,736
Here are the coefficient rows of the three sets. Row h n counts compositions with each part at least 2.

86
00:11:42,186 --> 00:11:45,834
Row j n counts compositions with each part odd.

87
00:11:46,284 --> 00:11:50,359
Row f n counts compositions with each part 1 or 2.

88
00:11:50,809 --> 00:12:03,054
Phi of H is 1 plus x squared times phi of F. Multiplication by x squared moves every coefficient of the f row two places to the right. The arrows show this.

89
00:12:03,504 --> 00:12:10,203
Phi of J is 1 plus x times phi of F. This moves the f row one place to the right.

90
00:12:10,653 --> 00:12:22,599
So h n equals j n minus 1, and both equal f n minus 2, for n at least 2. For example, h 7, j 6 and f 5 are all 8.

91
00:12:23,049 --> 00:12:37,236
Here are the three sets: H 7, J 6 and F 5. Each has 8 compositions. We proved this without a formula for the numbers. Exercise 2.17 asks for bijections between them.

92
00:12:37,686 --> 00:12:45,729
Example 2.22. Q is the set of compositions with each part at least 2 and an even number of parts.

93
00:12:46,179 --> 00:12:58,040
Step 1 is the same as for H in Example 2.19. P is the set 2, 3, 4, and so on, and phi of P is x squared over 1 minus x.

94
00:12:58,490 --> 00:13:08,495
An even length is 2 j. Group the parts in pairs, from the left. The composition 3, 2, 2, 4 has length 4, so it is 2 pairs.

95
00:13:08,945 --> 00:13:18,716
A composition of length 2 j is a list of j pairs. So it is an element of P squared, to the j. So Q is P squared, star.

96
00:13:19,166 --> 00:13:28,787
By the Product Lemma, phi of P squared is x squared over 1 minus x, squared. That is x to the 4 over 1 minus x, squared.

97
00:13:29,237 --> 00:13:37,216
Phi of P squared has no constant term. So by the String Lemma, phi of Q is 1 over 1 minus that series.

98
00:13:37,666 --> 00:13:49,357
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x, squared. Phi of Q is 1 minus x, squared, over 1 minus x, squared, minus x to the 4.

99
00:13:49,807 --> 00:14:05,849
To expand it, multiply out the bottom: 1 minus 2 x plus x squared minus x to the 4. Compare the coefficients. q n is 2 q n minus 1, minus q n minus 2, plus q n minus 4.

100
00:14:06,299 --> 00:14:11,121
At x to the 7: 2 times 3, minus 2, plus 0, is 4.

101
00:14:11,571 --> 00:14:19,677
Check by listing. The compositions of 7 in Q are 2 5, 3 4, 4 3 and 5 2. There are 4.

102
00:14:20,127 --> 00:14:30,965
Practice problem P3-5. Fix p at least 2. Count the compositions of n where every part is 1 or p. Pause the video and try it.

103
00:14:31,415 --> 00:14:37,324
Step 1. The allowed parts are 1 and p. Their series is x plus x to the p.

104
00:14:37,774 --> 00:14:41,337
Step 2. Any length is allowed, so the set is P star.

105
00:14:41,787 --> 00:14:53,776
Step 3. Phi of P has no constant term. By the String Lemma, the answer is the coefficient of x to the n in 1 over 1 minus x minus x to the p.

106
00:14:54,226 --> 00:15:05,554
Check the case p equal to 3 and n equal to 7. Here are the 9 compositions of 7 with parts 1 or 3. The orange blocks are parts equal to 3.

107
00:15:06,004 --> 00:15:23,412
Expand the series for p equal to 3. Multiply by 1 minus x minus x cubed, and compare the coefficients. The first three coefficients are 1. After them, each g n is g n minus 1 plus g n minus 3.

108
00:15:23,862 --> 00:15:31,158
At x to the 7: g 6 plus g 4 is 6 plus 3, which is 9. This matches the list.

109
00:15:31,608 --> 00:15:43,640
Practice problem P3-6. C is the set of compositions with each part at most 3. Here the weight is the length, not the size. Pause the video and try it.

110
00:15:44,090 --> 00:15:50,362
The parts are 1, 2 and 3. Each part adds 1 to the length. So each part has weight 1.

111
00:15:50,812 --> 00:15:55,100
So phi of P is x plus x plus x, which is 3 x.

112
00:15:55,550 --> 00:16:04,553
Here are the compositions of length 2. Each part has 3 choices, so there are 3 times 3, which is 9. Each has weight 2.

113
00:16:05,003 --> 00:16:09,397
The Product Lemma agrees: 3 x, squared, is 9 x squared.

114
00:16:09,847 --> 00:16:15,821
Any length is allowed. By the String Lemma, phi of C is 1 over 1 minus 3 x.

115
00:16:16,271 --> 00:16:29,412
By the geometric series, this is 1 plus 3 x plus 9 x squared, and so on. The coefficient of x to the k is 3 to the k. At x squared it is 9, as in the grid.

116
00:16:29,862 --> 00:16:40,465
With the size as the weight, the same set gives 1 over 1 minus x minus x squared minus x cubed. Always check which weight the question uses.

117
00:16:40,915 --> 00:16:49,811
Recap. A composition is a list of positive integers. Its length is the number of parts. Its size is the sum of the parts.

118
00:16:50,261 --> 00:17:02,783
All compositions form P star. The String Lemma gives 1 minus x over 1 minus 2 x. So there are 2 to the n minus 1 compositions of n, for n at least 1.

119
00:17:03,233 --> 00:17:13,516
The compositions of n with length k number n minus 1 choose k minus 1. Choose k minus 1 cuts among n minus 1 gaps.

120
00:17:13,966 --> 00:17:22,307
For a restricted set, find the allowed parts and their series. Then find the allowed lengths. Then apply the lemmas and name them.

121
00:17:22,757 --> 00:17:34,320
Any length gives 1 over 1 minus phi of P. Even length gives 1 over 1 minus phi of P squared. Length exactly k gives phi of P to the k.

122
00:17:34,770 --> 00:17:44,242
Always check the weight: size or length. Video 10b solves the tutorial, assignment and midterm problems on compositions.
